 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Though he’s just turned 30, the American in London Conner Ives has made quite a name for himself, becoming a viral sensation for his “Protect the Dolls” T-shirt last year and placing his cocktail dresses on a much-photographed swath of <em>Vanity Fair</em> Oscar Party guests back in March.</p><p>A big crowd of people turned up at The Grill on Saturday afternoon to see what all the buzz is about. Ives has shown at Claridge’s and the Savoy in London, so he isn’t afraid of a big room, but this was a debut and a homecoming rolled into one—he hails from Westchester County originally—and he confessed to nerves.</p><p>Not that you could tell from the clothes. “Passion and flamboyance are my most inherently American traits,” he declared in his show notes. Swapping continents was no reason to adjust his delivery. Ives’s high-low mix for spring ran from those photogenic embroidered satin frocks and repurposed vintage fur-trimmed robes and bed jackets on one side to color-blocked rugbies, slogan tees, and embellished jeans on the other.</p><p>The T-shirt Hari Nef modeled declared “Bring Back Shame.” Jacob Elordi advocated for the concept in a <em>Wall Street Journal</em> interview that stirred much debate. Does the designer intend it ironically? He must. The Conner Ives world contains multitudes, though not all of it is as persuasive as his slogan tees. The gilded matador looks leaned costumey, and the horsey motifs and bandana prints were too on the nose. But the watercolor prints of fish scales—“the final frontier of exotics” per the show notes—displayed a new level of ambition, and the same goes for a pair of men’s suits made in collaboration with the Savile Row tailor Gieves &amp; Hawkes. No doubt a good many of these pieces will make their off-runway debut at Ives’s after-party tonight.</p></div>",
